## IIS setup (not code — done once on the server, using Windows Server features)

1. **Install IIS** with these role services: **Windows Authentication**, **Application Request Routing (ARR)**, and **URL Rewrite** (ARR and URL Rewrite are separate downloads via Web Platform Installer if not already present).
2. **Create a new IIS site** for this app (pointing to any empty folder — IIS only proxies through it, it doesn't serve files directly).
3. In that site's **Authentication** settings: **disable Anonymous Authentication**, **enable Windows Authentication**.
4. In **URL Rewrite**, add an inbound rule that proxies all requests to the Spring Boot app:
   - Pattern: `(.*)`
   - Action: **Rewrite** to `http://127.0.0.1:8080/{R:1}`
5. Still in that rule (or as a separate outbound rule), add a **Server Variable** action:
   - Set `HTTP_X_REMOTE_USER` to `{LOGON_USER}`
   - (You'll first need to add `HTTP_X_REMOTE_USER` to ARR's allowed server variables list — IIS Manager → server-level ARR settings → **Server Proxy Settings** → add it there, or edit `applicationHost.config` directly if not visible in the UI.)
6. Confirm the Spring Boot app itself is **not directly reachable** from the network — with `server.address=127.0.0.1` in `application.properties`, it only listens on the local machine, so IIS (also on that machine) is the only path in. Employees hit IIS (port 80/443), never the raw Spring Boot port directly.

## Why `server.address=127.0.0.1` matters — security note

The header `X-Remote-User` is only trustworthy because **IIS** is the one setting it, after verifying the person's real Windows identity. If the Spring Boot app were still reachable directly on the network (its old default), anyone could bypass IIS entirely and send a request straight to the app with a **fake** `X-Remote-User` header claiming to be someone else — the app would trust it blindly, since it has no way to know the header didn't come from IIS. Binding the app to `127.0.0.1` closes that gap: it becomes physically unreachable except through IIS on the same machine.

## Before calling this production-ready

Test end-to-end from one real domain-joined employee desktop: open the IIS site's URL, confirm it lands directly on the survey with **no login prompt**, their code/name auto-filled, and their previous submission (if any) loads correctly for editing. Also verify directly hitting `http://<server>:8080` from another machine on the network fails/times out (confirming the app truly isn't reachable except through IIS).

**Also verify before go-live:** run a full `mvn compile` (or build in your IDE) after making all the above changes — the missing `findByCode` method is the kind of gap that only shows up as a compile error, not something you'd catch by re-reading the doc.